// =============================================================================
// memory.lumen — the Memory pack (the resource / relationship model)
// =============================================================================
// A BDP pack modelling arbitrary knowledge (Memory), obligations (Constraint) and
// assessments (Evaluation) on the 0.2.3 resource model, together with the six
// relationship types that connect them (§1.4) — context, retirement, dependency,
// defining structure, applicability, and the reified conformance claim.
// Companion to the Issue and stdlib packs.
//
// PRINCIPLES (as issue.lumen):
//   1. Model before commands. §1 models the resources; §2 derives the command
//      surface from them, never the reverse.
//   2. Refinement via `is`. A subtype refines its parent with `type X is T`;
//      `abstract type` marks a parent that is not directly instantiated — only
//      its concrete leaves are. The type graph is OPEN: a closed/exhaustive set
//      is a modelling intent, not an enforced closure.
//   3. Relationships are EDGE RESOURCES. A relationship between two resources is
//      a first-class `rel<F, T>` bead with its own identity, properties and
//      revision history (§1.4) — not an owned `ref<T>` property on an endpoint.
//      The same reading issue.lumen takes for `Dependency` (§1.2 there), and the
//      one the cited semantic authority takes for all nine of its Link Types.
//      A bead therefore declares NO relationship fields; the facts it wants to
//      read back are `computed` projections over the edges, revision-transparent
//      exactly as issue.lumen's `computed dependencies` is.
//
// SOURCES. The semantic authority for the types below is the accepted-cut Memory
// specification (BDP `94f699b947382e3a52385b1895a941575fcc08cb`,
// `specs/types/memory/memory.md`; seven Bead Types, nine Link Types), which
// `specs/deferred.md` (MEM-MECHANICS) still cites as normative and which was
// deleted from the tree as superseded by this pack. The command surface in §2 is
// derived from `docs/cli-design/memory-cli.md`, the canonical Memory CLI.
// =============================================================================

/// description: Markdown text — a nominal string (lowers to a nominal over string).
type markdown is string


// #############################################################################
// §1. The model
// #############################################################################

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §1.1  Memory — arbitrary knowledge (root; OPEN, directly instantiable)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: Memory — a unit of arbitrary knowledge. Plain memories exist and packs refine subtypes.
/// normative: `title` is a required nonblank display name; `body` is required Markdown and MAY be empty;
/// normative: `status` is an optional nonblank organization-defined label that carries NO built-in workflow,
/// normative: approval or lifecycle effect. (`remember` derives an omitted creation title from the body, but
/// normative: the stored property is required — see §2.B.)
/// normative: A Memory holds NO relationship fields — context, retirement and applicability are the
/// normative: `References`, `SupersededBy` and `SubjectTo` edge resources of §1.4. The three projections
/// normative: below are COMPUTED: derived from the edge graph and revision-transparent, so reading or
/// normative: recomputing one never advances the Memory's revision, emits no change event and never enters
/// normative: its content hash. They are reverse-lookup views, never written fields; author the edges.
type Memory is Bead {
  // All three render as their default flag spellings on the generic verbs
  // (`--title` / `--body` / `--status`); `remember` takes the body POSITIONALLY,
  // which is stated on that op (§2.B), not here.
  title:   string { minLength: 1 },
  body:    markdown,
  status?: string { minLength: 1 },

  // -- computed: outgoing edge projections (revision-transparent; §1.4) --
  computed references:   [ ref<References> ],
  computed supersededBy: [ ref<SupersededBy> ],   // ≤1 live (arity is §1.4's rule)
  computed subjectTo:    [ ref<SubjectTo> ],
}


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §1.2  Constraint — an obligation (abstract; four concrete subtypes)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: Constraint — an obligation. Abstract: only the concrete subtypes below are instantiated.
/// normative: A Constraint holds NO relationship fields: dependency and defining structure are the
/// normative: `Requires` and `Defines` edge resources of §1.4, and the projections below are COMPUTED
/// normative: (revision-transparent, never written). `requires` projects the OUTGOING Requires family and so
/// normative: includes every `Defines` edge; `defines` is the Defines-only subset; `definedBy` is the one
/// normative: INCOMING projection the ≤1-defining-parent rule makes single-valued.
/// normative: There is deliberately NO `appliesTo` projection. Applicability is declared by the SUBJECT
/// normative: (`SubjectTo`, source = the subject), so on this end it is a pure reverse lookup with no
/// normative: ownership — exactly what `subjects` (§2.C) reads. A stored `appliesTo` would reinstate the
/// normative: inverted ownership this pack corrected; see §1.4.
/// normative: An empty `body` does NOT establish that the constraint is satisfied. Every `requires` target,
/// normative: including every defining child, is NECESSARY for satisfying this constraint; the constraint's own
/// normative: body still applies, so children are never collectively SUFFICIENT unless that is stated
/// normative: explicitly, and a constraint with no children is not automatically satisfied.
abstract type Constraint is Memory {
  // -- computed: Requires-family edge projections (revision-transparent; §1.4) --
  computed requires:  [ ref<Requires> ],      // outgoing, family-wide (cycles ALLOWED — see §2.C)
  computed defines:   [ ref<Defines> ],       // outgoing, Defines only
  computed definedBy: [ ref<Defines> ],       // incoming; ≤1 live per child identity
  // >> OPEN — Q4. The authority selects an optional `applicability` predicate on Constraint
  // >> ("executable subject-selection predicate; concrete representation DEFERRED"). It is
  // >> deliberately NOT declared here: lowering it would mean choosing a predicate
  // >> representation, which is the deferred decision itself. Matches would add implicit
  // >> applicability WITHOUT filtering explicit declarations.
}
type Policy is Constraint {
  // >> OPEN — Q5. The authority gives Policy an optional `authority` ("URI or `{uri, revision}`
  // >> Reference ... identifies the person, role, or governing source that issued the rule").
  // >> Not declared: the type is a genuine fork. `ref<resource>` reads it as a BDP resource,
  // >> but a person or role generally is not one, and issue.lumen deliberately models
  // >> principals as native strings, NOT refs (`owner`/`assignee`, §1.1 there). It is a claim
  // >> of authority, not proof of approval, either way. No CLI flag surfaces it today.
}
type Requirement is Constraint {}
type Scenario is Constraint {}

/// description: Specification — an intended result, described well enough to be checked against.
/// normative: `version` is an author-chosen label, INDEPENDENT of bead revisions and of any source/build
/// normative: identifier. It carries no automatic increment and no ordering. No CLI flag surfaces it today.
type Specification is Constraint {
  version?: string { minLength: 1 },
}


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §1.3  Evaluation — an assessment over a subject group
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: Evaluation — an assessment over a NONEMPTY group of subject resources.
/// normative: Every claim an Evaluation owns concerns the WHOLE subject group COLLECTIVELY; there is no
/// normative: per-claim subset selector, and a collective claim does not distribute to an individual member.
/// normative: The first accepted Evaluation state BINDS that group to this bead identity, and the binding
/// normative: survives retyping away from Evaluation and back. A different group requires a new Evaluation
/// normative: identity. Array order is immaterial (hence `set`). Recording a claim revises the Evaluation,
/// normative: never an assessed subject; an Evaluation may itself be assessed by another Evaluation.
type Evaluation is Memory {
  // `minItems` (not `minSize`) is the JSON Schema 2020-12 keyword — see the lowering note below.
  subjects: set<ref<resource>> { minItems: 1 },
  // >> OPEN — Q3. The authority requires each subject to be an EXACT pin — an object with
  // >> required `uri` AND `revision` — and says a bare URI or an object without a revision is
  // >> a floating reference that MUST be refused, never silently resolved to a newer revision.
  // >> `set<ref<resource>>` cannot express "pinned ref REQUIRED": `ref<T>` lowers to
  // >> `ResourceRef`, whose `rev` is optional, so a floating subject validates today. Either
  // >> Lumen needs a pin-required ref form, or this pack needs its own nominal pinned-ref
  // >> type. Until then the exactness rule is stated here but NOT enforced by the schema.

  // -- computed: the claims this evaluation owns (revision-transparent; §1.4) --
  computed conformance: [ ref<Conformance> ],
}


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §1.4  The relationship types — edge resources (Principle 3)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Six relationship types, each a `rel<F, T>` bead: its own identity, its own
// revision history, its own `title`/`body`/`status`. `References` is the root and
// is itself instantiable; the other five refine it, directly or transitively, and
// inherit its three content properties. `source` is always the asserting end, so
// the DIRECTION below is normative, not presentational.
//
// NOT `unique`, unlike issue.lumen's `Dependency`. Every family here explicitly
// ALLOWS parallel edges over the same ordered pair — independently authored edges
// keep their own identities, reasons and revision selections, and repeated paths
// are not additional satisfaction. `unique` would key by (type, source, target)
// and collapse exactly those distinct claims, so each edge takes a surrogate id
// instead, which is what the creation commands' `--id`/`--id-root` expect (§2.D).
//
// ENDPOINT NARROWING. All five refinements restipulate narrower endpoints than
// `References`' `rel<Bead, Bead>` — `rel<Memory, Memory>`, `rel<Constraint,
// Constraint>`, `rel<Bead, Constraint>`, `rel<Evaluation, Constraint>` — because
// the authority's allowed source→target pairs differ per family. Whether a
// refinement MAY narrow a parent rel's endpoints is the same Lumen-scrub question
// issue.lumen raises on `ReadyAffecting is Dependency rel<Issue, Issue>`; the
// intent here is likewise the conformance list, not a new rule. It is recorded in
// one place rather than repeated on each of the five.
//
// >> OPEN — Q7 (new; raised by this ruling, not by it being wrong). The authority
// >> makes four of these families SOURCE-OWNED: "adding, changing, or deleting one
// >> revises the source", while the target keeps its own revision. Direct
// >> `References` alone is UNOWNED — "neither endpoint revision changes". That
// >> distinction is not expressible here. `rel<F, T>` calls `source` "the owning
// >> endpoint that asserts the relationship" but attaches no revision consequence,
// >> its modifier set is `unique`/`acyclic`/`symmetric`/`reflexive`, and the
// >> shipped precedent states the OPPOSITE rule: issue.lumen's `Dependency`
// >> normatively holds that creating, re-typing or deleting an edge "does NOT
// >> touch its endpoints' revisions". So either Core grows an ownership modifier
// >> (and `References` is the unowned default its four refinements override), or
// >> Memory's source-owned revision rule is dropped to match Dependency. The
// >> directions and arities below are unaffected either way; what turns on it is
// >> whether writing an edge advances its source's revision. No modifier is
// >> invented here, so the rule is stated per type and NOT enforced.

/// description: References — a relationship from a bead to useful context, carried as a first-class edge resource. The root of this pack's relationship family and the only one that is itself unowned.
/// normative: Adds an informational connection and claims NOTHING further — not satisfaction, not replacement.
/// normative: UNOWNED: changing it revises neither endpoint (see Q7). Parallel edges and cycles are allowed;
/// normative: equal endpoints on separate calls do NOT deduplicate. A self-edge by bead identity is refused
/// normative: (`rel` is irreflexive by default). Endpoint eligibility is CORRELATED, not a free pair: Memory→Memory,
/// normative: Memory→Issue, Issue→Memory, or any bead→Constraint. Lumen has no disjunctive endpoint-pair form,
/// normative: so `rel<Bead, Bead>` is the widest honest signature and the pair table is carried here as prose
/// normative: and enforced by `reference`'s `ineligible-endpoints` refusal (§2.D), NOT by the schema.
/// normative: `title`, `body` and `status` are each optional and NONBLANK, and belong to the edge, never to an
/// normative: endpoint; `status` is descriptive and activates, validates and invalidates nothing.
type References is rel<Bead, Bead> {
  title?:  string { minLength: 1 },
  body?:   markdown { minLength: 1 },
  status?: string { minLength: 1 },
}

/// description: SupersededBy — retires a Memory in favour of a replacement with a different identity. Source is the retiring bead, target its replacement.
/// normative: Expresses normative succession: readers follow the successor for current guidance. It does NOT
/// normative: delete the source, approve the replacement, set a status or rewrite the source's applicability
/// normative: declarations, and the retired bead and its history stay readable. Each source has ZERO OR ONE
/// normative: live outgoing edge: a second refuses even when it already names the same target (§2.D's
/// normative: `already-superseded`), and this command never retargets or treats an existing successor as a
/// normative: no-op. Endpoints are distinct Memory identities, both FLOATING — an exact pin is refused.
/// normative: `acyclic` restipulates `rel<Memory, Memory>`: following the chain must never revisit a bead.
/// normative: Source-owned per the authority (Q7). Arity and acyclicity are graph invariants the writer
/// normative: enforces; neither lowers to the schema (§1.5), so §2.D's refusals are where they are checked.
type SupersededBy is References, acyclic rel<Memory, Memory> {}

/// description: Requires — says the target Constraint is necessary for satisfying the source Constraint.
/// normative: Necessity composes TRANSITIVELY across the family, Defines included. Many outgoing and many
/// normative: incoming edges, no minimum and no maximum. CYCLES ARE ALLOWED — mutual necessity is meaningful,
/// normative: supplies no satisfaction evidence, and a traversal must terminate on revisited identities
/// normative: without treating a repeat as progress; so `acyclic` is deliberately NOT restipulated here.
/// normative: Parallel edges are allowed and repeated paths are not additional satisfaction. Recording every
/// normative: necessary target never becomes SUFFICIENT: the source's own body still applies. Requires alone
/// normative: assigns no defining parent. Source-owned per the authority (Q7).
type Requires is References rel<Constraint, Constraint> {}

/// description: Defines — makes the source Constraint the DEFINING PARENT of the target, and inherits Requires' necessity. Source is the parent, target the child.
/// normative: Supplies the defining tree. A parent may define many children; each CHILD identity has zero or
/// normative: one live INCOMING Defines-family edge, and a second refuses even from the same parent (§2.D's
/// normative: `already-defined`). That is a per-endpoint arity cap, not a natural-key rule, so `unique`
/// normative: cannot express it. `acyclic` restipulates `rel<Constraint, Constraint>` — the Defines family
/// normative: forbids cycles even though its Requires parent allows them. A standalone constraint is a legal
/// normative: root; no defining parent is required. The child keeps its own type, identity, properties and
/// normative: history: owning the edge transfers no ownership of the child and grants no right to edit or
/// normative: delete it, and deleting a parent implies no cascade. Another constraint may REQUIRE the child
/// normative: without becoming a second defining parent. Defined children are necessary by default, never
/// normative: collectively sufficient unless stated. Source-owned per the authority (Q7) — and this is the
/// normative: end the inversion corrected: the PARENT's revision moves, never the child's.
type Defines is Requires, acyclic rel<Constraint, Constraint> {}

/// description: SubjectTo — declares that the target Constraint applies to the source bead. Source is the SUBJECT, which is the end that owns the declaration.
/// normative: Identifies an applicable obligation and asserts NO contribution, satisfaction, verification or
/// normative: breach; adding or removing an assessment never removes the declaration. NOT TRANSITIVE: if X is
/// normative: subject to A and A to B, then B governs A, not X. Discover a subject's obligations by following
/// normative: each target's Requires family (Defines included) — a read creates no inferred stored edge.
/// normative: Many-to-many, parallel declarations may coexist, and a self-edge is refused by identity.
/// normative: Source-owned per the authority (Q7), and ownership is NOT conferred by endpoint eligibility:
/// normative: a source bead's own installed type and writer must declare it, so a source without that
/// normative: support is refused (§2.D's `source-lacks-ownership`) rather than silently accepted. Memory
/// normative: declares it for its family; Issue and other non-Memory sources must each add it, and widening
/// normative: endpoints alone installs nothing. A declaration is not proof of authority to bind the source.
/// normative: This is the second end the inversion corrected: the SUBJECT's revision moves, not the
/// normative: Constraint's, which is why no `appliesTo` survives on Constraint (§1.2).
type SubjectTo is References rel<Bead, Constraint> {}

/// description: Conformance — the abstract claim family: a claim by the source Evaluation about its FULL assessed subject group relative to the target Constraint. Abstract, so only the three concrete peers below are instantiated.
/// normative: The source Evaluation names the subjects, so a claim always concerns that evaluation's whole
/// normative: group COLLECTIVELY and cannot select a subset. The common type is NEUTRAL: membership in the
/// normative: family alone establishes no conclusion. `Addresses` claims a contribution without asserting
/// normative: completeness, `Satisfies` claims complete satisfaction, `Violates` claims a specific breach.
/// normative: The three are PEERS — `Violates` does NOT refine `Addresses`, and a breach establishes no
/// normative: contribution. Many-to-many; PARALLEL AND CONTRADICTORY claims from different evaluations about
/// normative: the same group and constraint each keep their own identity and MUST be exposed, never silently
/// normative: overwritten, resolved or counted as votes. There is no unowned claim form and no third-party
/// normative: owner: every claim is Evaluation-owned (Q7), and a mutation revises that evaluation, not its
/// normative: assessed subjects and not the target. No general transitivity or automatic coverage rule.
/// normative: `evidence` preserves test, review, execution or source pointers; neither it nor an authored
/// normative: claim is independent verification. The claiming actor rides in PROVENANCE and is distinct from
/// normative: the owning evaluation and its subjects. Abstractness here does not make a refinement abstract,
/// normative: and family queries and ownership selectors still match the concrete peers.
abstract type Conformance is References rel<Evaluation, Constraint> {
  evidence?: text { minLength: 1 },
}
type Addresses is Conformance {}
type Satisfies is Conformance {}
type Violates is Conformance {}


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §1.5  Lowering notes — what the schema does and does not enforce
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `{ minItems: 1 }` is a real JSON Schema 2020-12 array keyword and lowers to
// `"minItems": 1`. The previous spelling here, `{ minSize: 1 }`, was NOT: the
// generator's constraint parser passes unknown keys through verbatim (it maps only
// `min`/`max`), so it emitted `"minSize": 1`, which every validator IGNORES —
// Evaluation's nonempty-subjects rule was silently inert in the generated bundle.
// It was the only such key in all of specs/src.
//
// Still carried as modelling intent rather than enforced schema:
//   - `abstract` — no JSON Schema analogue; the bundle emits the concrete leaves
//     and the abstract parent alike.
//   - `acyclic` on `SupersededBy` and `Defines` — a graph invariant over many
//     documents, not a per-document constraint, so it is dropped in lowering.
//   - `References`' correlated endpoint-pair table — Lumen has no disjunctive
//     endpoint form (§1.4).
//   - the per-endpoint arity caps (≤1 outgoing successor, ≤1 incoming defining
//     parent) — `unique` keys by (type, source, target) and cannot express them.
//   - source-ownership of a relationship family, i.e. whether writing an edge
//     advances its source's revision — see Q7 (§1.4).
// Each is enforced instead by the writer, and surfaces as a declared domain
// refusal on the creating command where one exists (§2.D).
//
// TWO GENERATOR GAPS, both pre-existing and both shared with issue.lumen, so they
// are recorded rather than worked around — contorting a normative model to suit a
// lowering limitation would be the wrong trade:
//   - A REFINED relationship lowers to `$comment: "UNLOWERED: …"`. The generator
//     handles the bare `rel<F, T>` form (so `References` lowers), but not one
//     carrying a parent or a modifier, so `SupersededBy`, `Requires`, `Defines`,
//     `SubjectTo` and `Conformance` emit no schema. issue.lumen's shipped bundle
//     has the same five gaps for the same reason (`Dependency` among them, from
//     its `unique`). Flattening the hierarchy into five independent bare rels
//     would lower, and would destroy the refinement the authority requires —
//     every specialized type refines `references` — so it is not done. Note also
//     that even a lowered `rel` erases its endpoint types: `rel<F, T>` becomes a
//     `$ref` to core's `rel`, so the F/T pairing is never schema-checked.
//   - `computed` has no lowering. A computed field is emitted as an ordinary
//     property AND listed in `required`, which is wrong twice: a derived value is
//     neither authored nor mandatory on a document. issue.lumen's `isBlocked`,
//     `dependencies`, `labels` and `createdBy` lower the same way today. The
//     revision-transparency contract these fields rely on is therefore stated in
//     §1.1–§1.3 and honoured by the writer, not expressed in the bundle.


// #############################################################################
// §2. The command surface — derived from §1
// #############################################################################
// Five sections. §2.A is the generic verbs, which ARE core primitives and are NOT
// redefined here. §2.B is the four Memory commands. §2.C is the ten relationship
// traversals. §2.D is the five relationship-CREATION commands. §2.E records why
// claim authoring is absent. Queries are `fn` (safe); writes are `command`.
// Signatures model the SHAPE: generic shared flags (scope selection,
// revision guards, object inputs, `--output-format`/`--long`/`--json`/`--quiet`,
// and the `--body-file`/`--stdin` body-input spellings) are orthogonal plumbing
// owned by the general CLI and never appear here.
//
// CLI MOUNT: this pack mounts at `bd memory` — `cli.mount: "memory"` — so its ops
// render as `bd memory remember` / `bd memory references`. Each op's `/// cli.name`
// is relative to that mount. `cli.mount` is a PACK-level hint; until the
// pack-machinery spec defines a package-declaration anchor, it is stated here in
// prose rather than a structured line. The four §2.B ops additionally keep their
// trained top-level spellings (`bd remember` / `bd recall` / `bd memories` /
// `bd forget`) as compatibility aliases, carried as `/// cli.alias`; both spellings
// share one handler, and an alias is not a second contract.

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §2.A  Generic verbs → core primitives (rendered, never redefined)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   create  → create(Memory)            show    → get(ref<Memory>)
//   list    → list(Memory, where?)      update  → patch(ref<Memory>, …)
//   delete  → delete(ref<Memory>)       count   → list(Memory, where?):count
// The pack RENDERS the trained names; each lowers to the primitive beside it. The
// §2.B convenience commands are NOT replacements for these: `bd list --type memory`
// is the Type-family inventory with paging and full-resource output, while
// `memories` (§2.B) is a bounded compact summary view, and `show` returns the bead
// document while `recall` returns only the body. Neither substitutes for the other.

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §2.0  The error model — primitives fault; operations declare a Result<T, E>
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// As issue.lumen §2.0. A PRIMITIVE returns its value or faults with a `Problem`
// (the universal fault channel), declares no `E` and is not cancellable. An
// OPERATION settles as `Outcome<T, E> = Result<T, E> | fault | canceled`, so a
// command with its own domain "no" declares `Result<T, E>` — `E` is that op's
// DOMAIN refusals (business rules only). Not-found, wrong-selector, malformed
// input, flag conflicts and stale revision guards stay Problems and are NOT listed
// as domain errors: so `remember --update` naming a missing memory, a blank
// explicit creation title, two body sources at once, and an `--if-revision` miss
// are all Problems. Safe queries (`fn`) read-or-fault and carry no domain `E`;
// following stdlib's `graph`, a request exceeding an advertised traversal or
// acquisition bound FAULTS rather than silently truncating.

/// description: Domain refusals of `rememberMemory`.
/// normative: `already-exists` — `--create-only` named an already-allocated identity. `not-a-memory` — the
/// normative: identity resolves to a bead outside the Memory family (an Issue, say); an existing-ID write
/// normative: never silently retypes a bead. `identity-consumed` — the identity was used by a forgotten
/// normative: memory and is retained; it cannot be reused.
type RememberError = "already-exists" | "not-a-memory" | "identity-consumed"

/// description: Domain refusals of `forgetMemory`.
/// normative: `has-incident-links` — the memory still participates in a live relationship in EITHER
/// normative: direction; those must be removed through their own operations first. `not-a-memory` — the
/// normative: target is outside the Memory family. Deletion is a tombstone, so the identity and the earlier
/// normative: snapshots are retained; forgetting neither retires the memory in favour of a successor nor
/// normative: erases history.
type ForgetError = "has-incident-links" | "not-a-memory"


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §2.B  Memory commands (the four canonical Memory spellings)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// description: Store a Memory, or update the same Memory under an explicit identity — the create-or-update convenience over the generic `create`/`patch`. (`memory/remember`.)
/// normative: `key` chooses the literal public identity (the CLI spells it `--key` or the equivalent `--id`);
/// normative: it is NOT a title. It creates when unused and updates that same live memory when allocated.
/// normative: `update` instead REQUIRES an existing memory. The two are mutually exclusive, and `createOnly`
/// normative: requires one of them and refuses an allocated identity. With no explicit identity the id derives
/// normative: from the body by the trained content-to-key rule, and a single bare key-like argument naming an
/// normative: existing memory RECALLS it rather than writing.
/// normative: Creation needs exactly one body source. An omitted creation title derives from the body (first
/// normative: trimmed line, whitespace collapsed, at most 80 code points including the ellipsis when
/// normative: shortened; an empty summary becomes `Untitled memory`). An existing-ID write PRESERVES every
/// normative: omitted content property, and requires at least one actual edit input — a revision guard alone
/// normative: is not an edit and cannot authorize creation.
/// normative: `type` selects an installed Memory type or refinement and is accepted ONLY with `createOnly`;
/// normative: an existing-ID write preserves the bead's current type. Use the generic `update … --type` to
/// normative: retype. The content shorthand is limited to title/body/status; the full property-edit surface
/// normative: stays with the generic verb. This is a convenience over two primitives, NOT a portable atomic
/// normative: upsert: the writer decides the admitted route, and an unchanged state retains its revision.
/// cli.name: "remember"
/// cli.alias: remember
command rememberMemory(
    /// cli.positional: 0
    body?:       markdown,
    /// cli.flag: --key
    key?:        string,
    /// cli.flag: --update
    update?:     ref<Memory>,
    /// cli.flag: --type
    type?:       PathString,
    /// cli.flag: --title
    title?:      string,
    /// cli.flag: --status
    status?:     string,
    /// cli.flag: --create-only
    createOnly?: bool = false
  ) -> Result<rep<Memory>, RememberError>

/// description: Read a Memory's Markdown body — only the body, not the bead document. (`memory/recall`.)
/// normative: The reference may select a retained revision (`@N` / `@=TOKEN`), in which case that saved
/// normative: body is returned verbatim. The generic `show` returns the resource representation instead;
/// normative: `recall` never switches to it.
/// cli.name: "recall"
/// cli.alias: recall
fn recallMemory(
    /// cli.positional: 0
    memory: ref<Memory>
  ) -> markdown

/// description: Browse compact Memory summaries, optionally matching text in the id, title or body. (`memory/memories`.)
/// normative: `search` is ONE literal, case-insensitive id/title/body match — not a selector, regex, graph
/// normative: predicate or query language. Omitted, it browses the admitted population. `details` adds
/// normative: revision, attribution and owned-relationship counts, and obtains no neighbouring bodies.
/// normative: The view is BOUNDED and has no paging: a selection exceeding the default match bound FAULTS
/// normative: with a request to narrow the search or pass `all`, which keeps the acquisition and output
/// normative: caps. A bounded selection is returned COMPLETE or it fails — never the first N as though all
/// normative: matches had been reported. A successful empty selection stays distinguishable from a failure.
/// cli.name: "memories"
/// cli.alias: memories
fn listMemorySummaries(
    /// cli.positional: 0
    search?:  string,
    /// cli.flag: --all
    all?:     bool = false,
    /// cli.flag: --details
    details?: bool = false
  ) -> [ MemorySummary ]

/// description: MemorySummary — one row of the compact summary view: identity, title, a bounded body excerpt, and the optional `details` fields.
/// normative: The excerpt is positioned at the match when the body matched, and otherwise starts at the
/// normative: beginning. `revision`, `createdBy` and `linkCount` are present only under `details`.
/// normative: >> OPEN — Q6. The CLI specification marks the machine framing of this view Unreviewed and
/// normative: calls the existing shape experimental, so these field names are derived from its documented
/// normative: result prose ("identities, titles, bounded body excerpts and optional details" plus
/// normative: "revision, attribution and owned-Link counts"), not transcribed from a settled schema.
type MemorySummary = {
  memory:     ref<Memory>,
  title:      string,
  excerpt:    string,
  type:       PathString,
  revision?:  Rev,
  createdBy?: string,
  linkCount?: integer
}

/// description: Forget a Memory — delete one live, unreferenced Memory; the invocation itself supplies the delete intent. (`memory/forget`.)
/// normative: Requires a live memory with NO incident relationship in either direction; remove those first.
/// normative: There is no `--force`, preview mode or cascade here: the generic `delete` without `--force` is
/// normative: the observational preview, and it reserves no later successful deletion. Returns the terminal
/// normative: revision (the tombstone). The consumed identity and the earlier snapshots are retained, so the
/// normative: identity cannot be reused (see `RememberError.identity-consumed`).
/// cli.name: "forget"
/// cli.alias: forget
command forgetMemory(
    /// cli.positional: 0
    memory: ref<Memory>
  ) -> Result<Rev, ForgetError>


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §2.C  Relationship traversals — the ten bounded family reads
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Each op fixes ONE relationship family and ONE direction, so none takes a family
// filter or a direction flag; they are named entry points over the generic
// `stdlib/graph`, exactly as issue.lumen's `getDependencyTree` is. They return
// stdlib's `Subgraph` — the same shape the generic `graph` returns — because the
// CLI specification defines them as conveniences over it and requires the result to
// retain roots, per-root membership, supporting paths, the actual relationship type
// and original arrow direction (including on incoming reads), the depth frontier,
// and cycle-closing edges.
//
// SHARED SEMANTICS. Every read takes one or more roots and holds them in ONE
// consistent, bounded view. Roots with no matching relationship are PRESERVED.
// Each root gets its own hop allowance; acquisition, work and output caps are
// cumulative across all roots. For each root, find the beads reached in the fixed
// direction within the depth, then keep all matching-family edges AMONG THOSE BEADS;
// combine the per-root graphs WITHOUT inducing extra edges across their union (for
// A→B, A→C, B→D, C→D, roots A and B at depth 1 retain AB, AC and BD, not CD).
// Visited identities stop repeated expansion without dropping selected edges. Exact
// stored pins are preserved as stored and MUST NOT be floated, and current content
// MUST NOT be substituted for a historical root. A depth frontier is VISIBLE and
// proves nothing — not a terminal successor, not discovery of every governing rule,
// and not compliance. An invalid, inaccessible or unavailable root, an incompatible
// binding, or a budget failure REFUSES the aggregate; it never degrades to a
// successful empty or partial result. A read never repairs invalid stored state.
//
// EDGE IDENTITY. `Subgraph`'s `GraphEdge` carries `edge: ref<resource>` and
// `type: PathString`. Under Principle 3 both are populated: every edge these reads
// traverse IS a §1.4 relationship resource, so `edge` reports its own id and `type`
// its concrete relationship type — which is what lets a reader distinguish parallel
// edges over one ordered pair, and what the CLI's requirement to report "the actual
// relationship type and original arrow direction, including on incoming reads"
// needs. An incoming read reports the edge as STORED (source→target), never flipped
// to match the traversal direction.

/// description: Follow outgoing References from each root to find related context. (`memory/references`.)
/// cli.name: "references"
fn listReferences(
    /// cli.positional: 0
    roots:  [ ref<resource> ],
    /// cli.alias: -d
    /// cli.flag: --depth
    depth?: integer = 1
  ) -> Subgraph

/// description: Follow incoming References to find the beads that refer to each root. (`memory/referenced-by`.)
/// cli.name: "referenced-by"
fn listReferencedBy(
    /// cli.positional: 0
    roots:  [ ref<resource> ],
    /// cli.alias: -d
    /// cli.flag: --depth
    depth?: integer = 1
  ) -> Subgraph

/// description: Follow the replacement chain FORWARD from each retiring Memory to its current successor. (`memory/successor`.)
/// normative: Succession is normative: readers should follow the successor for current guidance. The chain is
/// normative: acyclic and each memory has at most one live replacement, so the default depth is the 1000-hop
/// normative: bound rather than a single hop. Reaching the frontier does NOT prove a terminal successor.
/// cli.name: "successor"
fn getSuccessorChain(
    /// cli.positional: 0
    roots:  [ ref<resource> ],
    /// cli.alias: -d
    /// cli.flag: --depth
    depth?: integer = 1000
  ) -> Subgraph

/// description: Follow the replacement chain BACKWARD from a replacement, showing its direct predecessors by default. (`memory/supersedes`.)
/// cli.name: "supersedes"
fn listSupersedes(
    /// cli.positional: 0
    roots:  [ ref<resource> ],
    /// cli.alias: -d
    /// cli.flag: --depth
    depth?: integer = 1
  ) -> Subgraph

/// description: Follow the outgoing Requires FAMILY — including Defines — to collect the constraints necessary for each root. (`memory/requirements`.)
/// normative: Necessity composes TRANSITIVELY across the family, so the default depth is the 1000-hop bound.
/// normative: Cycles are ALLOWED in this family — mutual necessity is meaningful — so traversal MUST
/// normative: terminate on repeated visits without treating a repeated path as additional satisfaction. A
/// normative: cycle supplies no satisfaction evidence. Defines children are rendered before other Requires
/// normative: dependencies at a constraint.
/// cli.name: "requirements"
fn listRequirements(
    /// cli.positional: 0
    roots:  [ ref<resource> ],
    /// cli.alias: -d
    /// cli.flag: --depth
    depth?: integer = 1000
  ) -> Subgraph

/// description: Follow the incoming Requires family to find the constraints that depend on each root, showing direct dependents by default. (`memory/required-by`.)
/// cli.name: "required-by"
fn listRequiredBy(
    /// cli.positional: 0
    roots:  [ ref<resource> ],
    /// cli.alias: -d
    /// cli.flag: --depth
    depth?: integer = 1
  ) -> Subgraph

/// description: Follow outgoing Defines to read each root's defining parts — its defining tree. (`memory/definition`.)
/// normative: The defining tree is ACYCLIC and each child has at most one defining parent, so the default
/// normative: depth is the 1000-hop bound. A defining parent owns the relationship, not the child: the child
/// normative: keeps its own type, identity, properties and history, and deleting a parent does not imply
/// normative: deleting its children.
/// cli.name: "definition"
fn getDefinition(
    /// cli.positional: 0
    roots:  [ ref<resource> ],
    /// cli.alias: -d
    /// cli.flag: --depth
    depth?: integer = 1000
  ) -> Subgraph

/// description: Follow incoming Defines to find each root's defining parent, showing the direct parent by default. (`memory/defined-by`.)
/// normative: At most one live defining parent per child identity. A constraint may REQUIRE a child without
/// normative: becoming a second defining parent.
/// cli.name: "defined-by"
fn listDefinedBy(
    /// cli.positional: 0
    roots:  [ ref<resource> ],
    /// cli.alias: -d
    /// cli.flag: --depth
    depth?: integer = 1
  ) -> Subgraph

/// description: Discover the rules governing each subject — its explicit Subject To declarations, then the Requires-family paths that follow from them. (`memory/constraints`.)
/// normative: STAGED, not one traversal: first the subject's explicit declarations, then each declared
/// normative: constraint's Requires family (including Defines). Selecting both families in a single generic
/// normative: traversal is NOT equivalent. Subject To is NOT transitive — if X is subject to A and A is
/// normative: subject to B, then B governs A, not X — and shared expansion MUST NOT transfer a governing rule
/// normative: from one original subject to another. The read creates no inferred stored declaration.
/// normative: Discovery helps readers find the rules; it neither proves compliance nor affects issue
/// normative: readiness or scheduling.
/// normative: Takes NO depth: its staged bound has separate depth controls that remain unselected.
/// cli.name: "constraints"
fn listConstraints(
    /// cli.positional: 0
    subjects: [ ref<resource> ]
  ) -> Subgraph

/// description: Find the beads that DIRECTLY declare themselves subject to each selected Constraint. (`memory/subjects`.)
/// normative: Direct only — it takes no depth and performs no Requires-family expansion.
/// cli.name: "subjects"
fn listSubjects(
    /// cli.positional: 0
    constraints: [ ref<resource> ]
  ) -> Subgraph


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §2.D  Relationship creation — one command per family
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Five commands, each creating one §1.4 edge resource and returning it. Each takes
// its two endpoints POSITIONALLY; the CLI also accepts `--from`/`--to` for the same
// two parameters and all three spellings select the same operation, so the
// alternates are plumbing and are not modelled. Creation ids (`--id`/`--id-root`),
// object inputs (`--properties`/`--metadata`), scope selection, output format and
// the `--if-source-revision` guard are generic shared flags per §2's preamble and
// never appear in these signatures; a stale guard is a Problem, not a domain error.
//
// NO DELETE COUNTERPARTS. An earlier draft of this section expected five; the CLI
// specification has none. A relationship is removed with the generic `delete`
// primitive (§2.A) against the edge's own id — which is available precisely because
// an edge is a resource. `forgetMemory`'s `has-incident-links` refusal (§2.0) is
// what makes that ordering mandatory: remove the edges, then forget the bead.
//
// ERROR MODEL. Per §2.0, only DOMAIN refusals are listed. Endpoint eligibility,
// per-endpoint arity caps, prospective cycles and missing source ownership are
// business rules of the relationship model, so they are `E`. Deliberately NOT
// domain errors, and left as Problems: an unresolvable or inaccessible endpoint,
// a stale `--if-source-revision`, duplicate endpoint bindings, an owner guard
// passed to the unowned `reference`, and an endpoint PIN where the family admits
// only floating selection — a pin is a selector form, and §2.0 keeps
// wrong-selector faults in the Problem channel. That last one is the judgement
// call in this classification; it is called out so a reviewer can take it the
// other way.

/// description: Domain refusals of `createReference`.
/// normative: `ineligible-endpoints` — the pair is outside §1.4's correlated table (Memory→Memory,
/// normative: Memory→Issue, Issue→Memory, any bead→Constraint). `self-link` — both endpoints are the same
/// normative: bead identity, including across different revision selectors.
type ReferenceError = "ineligible-endpoints" | "self-link"

/// description: Domain refusals of `supersedeMemory`.
/// normative: `already-superseded` — the source already has a live outgoing successor; it refuses even when
/// normative: that successor is the target already, because the command never retargets and never reports a
/// normative: no-op. `would-cycle` — the edge would close a cycle in the succession graph.
type SupersedeError = "ineligible-endpoints" | "self-link" | "already-superseded" | "would-cycle"

/// description: Domain refusals of `createRequires`.
/// normative: There is deliberately NO `would-cycle`: a cycle through distinct Constraint identities is
/// normative: ALLOWED in the Requires family (§1.4), so it is not an error here.
type RequiresError = "ineligible-endpoints" | "self-link"

/// description: Domain refusals of `createDefines`.
/// normative: `already-defined` — the child already has a live incoming Defines-family edge; it refuses even
/// normative: from the same parent. `would-cycle` — the edge would close a cycle in the defining tree, which
/// normative: Defines forbids even though its Requires parent permits cycles.
type DefinesError = "ineligible-endpoints" | "self-link" | "already-defined" | "would-cycle"

/// description: Domain refusals of `createSubjectTo`.
/// normative: `source-lacks-ownership` — the source bead's installed type and authoritative writer do not
/// normative: declare outgoing SubjectTo ownership. Endpoint eligibility does NOT confer it, so this is a
/// normative: refusal and not a silent acceptance; `ineligible-endpoints` covers a target outside Constraint.
type SubjectToError = "ineligible-endpoints" | "self-link" | "source-lacks-ownership"

/// description: Create a References edge from a bead to useful context. (`memory/reference`.)
/// normative: Unowned — neither endpoint's revision changes. Parallel edges are allowed and equal endpoints
/// normative: on separate calls do NOT deduplicate, so a repeat call creates a SECOND edge rather than
/// normative: upserting the first. Both endpoints stay in one scope under the current proposal.
/// cli.name: "reference"
command createReference(
    /// cli.positional: 0
    source:  ref<Bead>,
    /// cli.positional: 1
    target:  ref<Bead>,
    title?:  string { minLength: 1 },
    body?:   markdown { minLength: 1 },
    status?: string { minLength: 1 }
  ) -> Result<rep<References>, ReferenceError>

/// description: Retire a Memory in favour of a replacement — creates the SupersededBy edge from the old memory to the new. (`memory/supersede`.)
/// normative: `old` is the retiring memory and `new` its replacement, which may be any Memory subtype. Both
/// normative: are floating selections. Retirement leaves `old` readable, deletes nothing, approves nothing,
/// normative: sets no status and rewrites no applicability declarations.
/// cli.name: "supersede"
command supersedeMemory(
    /// cli.positional: 0
    old:     ref<Memory>,
    /// cli.positional: 1
    new:     ref<Memory>,
    title?:  string { minLength: 1 },
    body?:   markdown { minLength: 1 },
    status?: string { minLength: 1 }
  ) -> Result<rep<SupersededBy>, SupersedeError>

/// description: Declare that the source Constraint depends on the target — creates the Requires edge. (`memory/requires`.)
/// normative: States necessity only. It does not execute either constraint, prove satisfaction, or make the
/// normative: target a defining child. The target stays independently maintained.
/// cli.name: "requires"
command createRequires(
    /// cli.positional: 0
    source:  ref<Constraint>,
    /// cli.positional: 1
    target:  ref<Constraint>,
    title?:  string { minLength: 1 },
    body?:   markdown { minLength: 1 },
    status?: string { minLength: 1 }
  ) -> Result<rep<Requires>, RequiresError>

/// description: Make the parent Constraint the defining parent of the child — creates the Defines edge. (`memory/defines`.)
/// normative: `parent` is the source and owns the edge; `child` is the target and keeps its own identity,
/// normative: type, properties and history. Defining children does not assert that they exhaust the parent's
/// normative: obligations — the parent's own body still applies.
/// cli.name: "defines"
command createDefines(
    /// cli.positional: 0
    parent:  ref<Constraint>,
    /// cli.positional: 1
    child:   ref<Constraint>,
    title?:  string { minLength: 1 },
    body?:   markdown { minLength: 1 },
    status?: string { minLength: 1 }
  ) -> Result<rep<Defines>, DefinesError>

/// description: Declare that a Constraint applies to a subject bead — creates the SubjectTo edge from the subject. (`memory/subject-to`.)
/// normative: `subject` is the source and the owning end. The declaration neither executes the constraint nor
/// normative: establishes compliance, and it does not change issue readiness or scheduling.
/// cli.name: "subject-to"
command createSubjectTo(
    /// cli.positional: 0
    subject:    ref<Bead>,
    /// cli.positional: 1
    constraint: ref<Constraint>,
    title?:     string { minLength: 1 },
    body?:      markdown { minLength: 1 },
    status?:    string { minLength: 1 }
  ) -> Result<rep<SubjectTo>, SubjectToError>

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// §2.E  Claim authoring — out of scope here
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The Conformance family (§1.4) has no authoring command in this pack. That is the
// CLI specification's own boundary — "Assessment authoring, evaluation/claim
// commands … are outside this companion" — and `specs/deferred.md`
// (MEM-MECHANICS) defers Memory's concrete query/CLI design to the Memory and
// shared Core/Types/Write owners, after Callable AND Package. The edges are
// MODELLED above because the model is what Principle 1 derives commands from; the
// commands that write them are a separate design pass. Creating one meanwhile is
// the generic `create` over the relationship type (§2.A).

// #############################################################################
// Appendix. What the edge-resource ruling changed
// #############################################################################
// Recorded because it is a NORMATIVE model change, not a refactor, and a reviewer
// should be able to see its extent without diffing two drafts.
//
//   1. Principle 3 inverted. "Relationships are refs; there is no Link" became
//      "relationships are edge resources". The old principle was a pre-0.2.3
//      leftover: `core.lumen` already carried `rel<F, T> is Bead { source, target }`
//      and issue.lumen already modelled `Dependency` as an edge.
//
//   2. Five ref properties removed, replaced by six relationship types (§1.4) and
//      `computed` projections (§1.1, §1.2, §1.3):
//        Memory.references    → References edge     + computed references
//        Memory.supersededBy  → SupersededBy edge   + computed supersededBy
//        Constraint.requires  → Requires edge       + computed requires / defines
//        Constraint.definedBy → Defines edge        + computed definedBy (INCOMING)
//        Constraint.appliesTo → SubjectTo edge      + NO projection on this end
//
//   3. Two ownership directions corrected against the authority's ownership table.
//      Both were stored on the end that does NOT own the relationship, and since
//      ownership determines whose revision advances, each was backwards:
//        `appliesTo` put the subject list on the CONSTRAINT, so a subject's
//        applicability declaration would have revised the constraint it declares
//        itself bound by. The authority: every source bead owns its outgoing
//        SubjectTo. Hence `SubjectTo`'s source is the SUBJECT, and the constraint
//        end is a pure reverse lookup — the `subjects` read (§2.C), not a field.
//        `definedBy` put the defining-parent pointer on the CHILD, so a parent's
//        decomposition choice would have revised the child. The authority: the
//        parent owns its outgoing Defines. Hence `Defines`' source is the PARENT,
//        and `definedBy` survives only as an incoming projection.
//
//   4. Conformance stopped being a bead. It was `is Bead` with `constraint` and
//      `evaluation` ref properties; those two properties ARE the edge's endpoints,
//      so it is now `abstract type Conformance is References rel<Evaluation,
//      Constraint>` carrying the authority's `evidence`. As a bead the rule that an
//      evaluation owns its claims was unstated and unenforceable.
//
//   5. §2.D's five creation commands became ordinary `command`s, and
//      `GraphEdge.edge` (§2.C) acquired a value to report.
//
// Q1 and Q2 of the previous draft are closed by this change. Q3–Q6 remain open and
// are untouched by it; Q7 (§1.4) is new and is raised BY it.
